\title{Using \texttt{gpim}}
\author{}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

<<setup,include=FALSE>>=
library(gpim)
options(width = 72)
@

\section{Data and endpoint order}

The package fits probabilistic index regression for prioritized survival
outcomes using the censoring adjustments of Cao, Fang, and Li~\cite{Cao2026}. Each subject contributes one terminal record and a separate record
for each observed nonfatal event. Code 0 denotes censoring, code 1 denotes
death, and codes 2, 3, and higher denote nonfatal outcomes in decreasing
priority. Baseline covariates are repeated consistently within a subject.
Follow-up continues after a nonfatal event.

The two supplied data sets, \texttt{hfaction} and \texttt{hierarchy}, are
synthetic. Neither contains original clinical participant records.
The \texttt{hfaction} data contain death and first hospitalization, with time
measured in years. The \texttt{hierarchy} data contain three endpoint priorities.

<<data,results=hide>>=
data(hfaction)
data(hierarchy)
@

The comparison begins with restricted death times. The next endpoint is
considered only after all higher priorities have been established as tied.
An unresolved comparison caused by censoring is distinct from a genuine tie.
The default order is increasing positive status code. An explicit
\texttt{priorities} vector must start with 1 and include every observed
positive code. Later event times are favorable.

Each nonfatal endpoint is time to its first event. With repeated records of
one type, \texttt{recurrent = "first"} uses the earliest occurrence and warns.
The option \texttt{recurrent = "error"} rejects repeated records.
Covariates must be constant within subject. Missing required values cause
an error by default. Setting \texttt{na.action = "na.omit"} removes the
whole subject.

\section{Fitting}

The formula uses differences between subject model-matrix rows. A free
intercept therefore cancels. Factors, transformations, and interactions
follow the usual R formula conventions.

<<fit>>=
fit <- gpim(
  HierSurv(time, status) ~
    treatment + I(age / 10) + sex + I(lvef / 5),
  data = hfaction,
  id = "id",
  censor_formula = ~ treatment + I(age / 10) + sex,
  method = c("CH", "HT", "DL"),
  link = "logit"
)
@

The age coefficient refers to a ten-year increase. The ejection fraction
coefficient refers to a five-percentage-point increase. Positive coefficients
favor subjects with a higher value of the corresponding predictor.

The default restriction times are the 25th, 50th, and 75th sample quantiles
of the observed terminal times, counting each subject once.
A positive vector supplied through \texttt{L} replaces these defaults.
The largest requested time must not exceed the largest observed terminal time.
Inference treats the selected times as fixed and intervals are pointwise.

<<restriction>>=
fit$L
@

CH requires both restricted individual histories to be observed. HT and DL use
comparisons that can be determined earlier. HT weights the centered outcome,
whereas DL weights the full residual. All three untruncated estimating
equations target the same full-data regression parameter under the stated
censoring assumptions.

The available links are \texttt{"identity"}, \texttt{"logit"}, and
\texttt{"probit"}. For a difference $z$ between outcome model-matrix rows,
write $\eta=z^\top\beta$. The means are $(1+\eta)/2$,
$\operatorname{expit}(\eta)$, and $\Phi(\eta)$, respectively.
All three satisfy $h(-\eta)=1-h(\eta)$.
Identity coefficients are on the conditional net-benefit scale. Identity
estimates at the probability boundary do not receive regular Wald inference.

A vector requests several links in the same call. For example,

<<links,eval=FALSE>>=
fit_links <- gpim(
  HierSurv(time, status) ~
    treatment + I(age / 10) + sex + I(lvef / 5),
  data = hfaction,
  censor_formula = ~ treatment + I(age / 10) + sex,
  method = "DL",
  link = c("identity", "logit", "probit")
)
@

The censoring model is a Cox model with Breslow handling of ties. Its
predictors are given by \texttt{censor\_formula}, which defaults to the outcome
formula's predictors. Use \texttt{censor\_formula = \textasciitilde{} 1}
for an unconditional censoring estimate. Weights use the left limit of the
cumulative censoring hazard.

\section{Results}

The summary provides coefficients, standard errors, confidence intervals,
and p-values. Standard extractors and selection by method, link, or restriction
time are available.

<<summary>>=
summary(fit, method = "DL", L = fit$L[2])
@

<<extract,eval=FALSE>>=
coef(fit)
vcov(fit, method = "DL", L = fit$L[2])
confint(fit, method = "DL")
trajectory_data(fit)
saveRDS(fit, "fit.rds")
@

The covariance calculation accounts for overlapping subject pairs and
estimation of both the censoring coefficients and baseline cumulative hazard.
It assumes independent subjects and a suitable censoring model with adequate
observation probabilities.

\section{Convergence controls}

The main controls are the iteration limit \texttt{maxit}, score tolerance
\texttt{tol}, relative step tolerance \texttt{step\_tol}, maximum Newton step
\texttt{max\_step}, and line-search limit \texttt{max\_halving}.
Censoring-model controls are \texttt{cox\_maxit}, \texttt{cox\_tol}, and
\texttt{cox\_score\_tol}.

<<control,eval=FALSE>>=
control <- pim_control(
  maxit = 200,
  tol = 1e-8,
  step_tol = 1e-10,
  cox_maxit = 100,
  trace = TRUE,
  progress = TRUE,
  on_failure = "warn"
)
@

For all nonlinear links, convergence requires the norm of the mean pair score
to be no greater than \texttt{tol}. A small step without meeting that
condition is reported as stagnation. Identity fitting is algebraic when
the root is admissible.

Named starting coefficients can be supplied through \texttt{start}.
The same vector is used for each requested nonlinear fit.
Within \texttt{pim\_control()}, \texttt{trace = TRUE} prints iterations,
while \texttt{progress = TRUE} reports progress through the requested fits.

The default failure policy, \texttt{"warn"}, retains problematic fits with
a warning. The \texttt{"quiet"} policy retains the results and messages
without a fitting warning. The \texttt{"stop"} policy stops when a requested
fit needs attention. Input errors always stop.

<<diagnostics>>=
d <- pim_diagnostics(fit)
d[, c("L", "method", "converged", "iterations", "score_norm")]
@

Convergence and availability of inference are recorded separately.
The diagnostic table also reports comparison counts and censoring weights.
Individual fits retain detailed iteration histories.

<<inspect,eval=FALSE>>=
subset(d, requires_attention)
fit$fits[[1]]$iteration_history
@

The default \texttt{weight\_floor = 0} leaves observation probabilities
untruncated. A positive floor changes the weights and can change the target.
Use \texttt{keep\_pairs = TRUE} to retain pair records for inspection.

\section{Coefficient trajectories}

The plot method displays each coefficient over the restriction times.
Shaded intervals are pointwise confidence intervals.

<<trajectory,fig=TRUE,width=7,height=6>>=
plot(fit, method = "DL")
@

The \texttt{parameters}, \texttt{method}, and \texttt{link} arguments select
the displayed coefficients and models. The \texttt{conf.int} argument
controls whether intervals are shown.
